<title>USA: Vermont contractors urge higher gasoline taxes.</title>
<headline>Vermont contractors urge higher gasoline taxes.</headline>
<dateline>NEW YORK 1997-01-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Vermont chapter of Associated General Contractors, saying that higher gasoline taxes could help improve Vermont's aging roads and bridges, said 80 percent of Vermont's roads and bridges are rated in fair, poor, or deficient condition.</p>
<p>The group cited federal surveys and a study by the Connecticut Policy and Economic Council.</p>
<p>The contractors contrasted Vermont's low gasoline taxes and poor roads to the state of Connecticut, which has the nation's highest gas taxes. Only 31 percent of Connecticut bridges are considered deficient and 43 percent of the state's highways are in fair or poor condition.</p>
<p>((--U.S. Municipal Desk, 212-859-1650))</p>
